Enforcement Decree of the Integrated Defense Act

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS <Newly Inserted on November 17, 2009>

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Integrated Defense Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Establishment of an Integrated Defense Posture, etc.)
(1) Pursuant to Article 3 (1) of the Integrated Defense Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"), the head of each central government office shall formulate necessary policies for fostering national defense elements and establishing an integrated defense posture, in accordance with the affairs under his or her jurisdiction and as prescribed by the President.
(2) Pursuant to Article 3 (2) of the Act, the head of each local government shall, in consultation with relevant authorities, including the regional military commander and the commissioner of the City/Do police agency, formulate necessary policies, including the preparation of directives necessary for regional integrated defense, to establish an integrated defense posture within the relevant jurisdiction. <Amended on December 31, 2020>

Article 3 (Holding of Integrated Defense Conferences, etc.)
(1) The chairperson of the Central Integrated Defense Council under Article 4 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Central Council") shall hold a central integrated defense conference at least once a year to evaluate, for each national defense element, the implementation of policies formulated under Article 3 (1) of the Act and Article 2 (1) of this Decree, and to establish an integrated defense posture. <Amended on August 20, 2013>
(2) The following persons shall be eligible to attend the central integrated defense conference: <Amended on November 19, 2014; July 26, 2017; December 24, 2018; December 31, 2020; March 23, 2021; October 1, 2025>
1. The chairperson and members of the Central Council;
2. The chairperson of the Broadcasting, Media and Communications Commission;
3. Chairpersons of the integrated defense councils for the Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan Cities, Special Self-Governing City, Dos, and Special Self-Governing Provinces under Article 5 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do Councils");
4. Heads of branch offices of the National Intelligence Service;
5. The Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency and commissioners of City/Do police agencies;
6. The Commissioner of the National Fire Agency and chiefs of fire headquarters;
7. The Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard and commissioners of regional coast guard agencies;
8. Military commanders at corps commander level or above;
9. Regional military commanders and fleet commanders;
10. Other persons selected by the Chief (hereinafter referred to as the "Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters") of the Integrated Defense Headquarters under Article 8 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Integrated Defense Headquarters").
(3) The Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall select the agenda, eligible attendees, date and time, venue, and presiding person for the central integrated defense conference and prepare for the conference.
(4) The chairperson of a City/Do Council shall hold a local integrated defense conference at least once a year to evaluate, for each national defense element, the implementation of policies formulated under Article 3 (2) of the Act and Article 2 (2) of this Decree, and to establish an integrated defense posture.
(5) The chairperson of a City/Do Council shall prepare for the conference in consultation with the heads of relevant authorities, including the regional military commander, the commissioner of the City/Do police agency, and the head of the branch office of the National Intelligence Service. <Amended on December 31, 2020>
(6) The Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall issue guidelines necessary for preparing for and holding local integrated defense conferences under paragraph (4). <Amended on July 2, 2019>

Article 4 (Support for Mobilization Expenses)
The scope of mobilization expenses for which the Government may provide support to local governments under Article 3 (4) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. Meal expenses for reserve forces mobilized for integrated defense operations;
2. Other expenses resolved upon by the Central Council.

CHAPTER II OPERATION OF INTEGRATED DEFENSE ORGANIZATIONS <Newly Inserted on November 17, 2009>

Article 5 (Members of the Central Council)
"Persons prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 4 (2) of the Act means persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Persons commissioned by the chairperson of the Central Council from among the heads of local governments;
2. Other persons appointed or commissioned by the chairperson of the Central Council from among persons with extensive knowledge and experience concerning integrated defense.

Article 6 (Matters for Deliberation by the Central Council)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 4 (4) 4 of the Act means the following:
1. Coordination of affairs related to integrated defense among government ministries and relevant authorities;
2. Mobilization expenses under Article 4;
3. Other agenda items submitted by members of the Central Council.

Article 7 (Convening of the Central Council, etc.)
(1) A meeting of the Central Council shall be convened when the chairperson deems it necessary.
(2) Unless otherwise expressly provided in this Decree, resolutions at a meeting of the Central Council shall be adopted with the attendance of a majority of its incumbent members and the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.

Article 8 (Standards for Ordinances on the Composition of Regional Councils, etc.)
(1) City/Do Councils and Si/Gun/Gu integrated defense councils under Article 5 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "Regional Councils") shall consist of the following persons: <Amended on August 20, 2013; November 19, 2014; January 6, 2015; July 26, 2017; August 21, 2018; October 7, 2020; December 31, 2020; November 1, 2022>
1. The commander of the military unit responsible for operations in the relevant region;
2. The commander of the Defense Counterintelligence Command unit in the relevant region or a member of that unit;
3. An official of the National Intelligence Service;
4. The chief prosecutor of a district prosecutors' office, the head of a branch office thereof, or a prosecutor;
5. The commissioner of a City/Do police agency or the chief of a police station;
6. The commissioner of a regional coast guard agency or the chief of a coast guard station;
7. The head of a regional corrections agency or the head of a correctional facility;
8. The head of a regional patriots and veterans affairs office or a branch office thereof;
9. The head of a regional military manpower office or a branch office thereof;
10. The superintendent of education or the head of a district education office;
11. The chairperson of the local council;
12. The chief of fire headquarters or the chief of a fire station;
13. The chairperson of the local veterans association;
14. Other persons commissioned by the chairperson of the Regional Council who have extensive knowledge and experience concerning integrated defense.
(2) Meetings of a Regional Council shall be classified as regular meetings and extraordinary meetings. In principle, regular meetings shall be convened once each quarter, and extraordinary meetings shall be convened when the chairperson deems it necessary. Article 7 (2) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the quorum for resolutions.
(3) Meetings of a Regional Council may be conducted by videoconference (including electronic voting). In such cases, participants in the videoconference shall be deemed present in the same meeting room. <Newly Inserted on December 24, 2018>
(4) To efficiently handle the affairs of a Regional Council, a Regional Integrated Defense Working Committee (hereinafter referred to as a "Regional Working Committee") shall be established under the Regional Council to perform the following functions: <Amended on December 24, 2018>
1. Preliminary deliberation on agenda items to be submitted to a meeting of the Regional Council;
2. Deliberation on matters delegated by the chairperson of the Regional Council;
3. Cooperation and coordination concerning integrated defense affairs among relevant administrative agencies.
(5) In principle, a Regional Working Committee shall be convened at least once each quarter. Matters necessary for its composition and operation shall be determined by the chairperson of the Regional Council following deliberation by the Regional Council. <Amended on December 24, 2018>
(6) Integrated defense preparedness measures under Article 5 (3) 2 of the Act shall include matters concerning national security education for local residents, students, and others and measures to support such education. <Amended on December 24, 2018>
(7) Measures to support integrated defense operations and training under Article 5 (3) 4 of the Act shall include the following: <Amended on November 29, 2016; December 24, 2018>
1. Measures to provide vehicles, vessels, facilities, and other support when integrated defense operations are conducted;
2. Publicity, awareness-raising, and support measures to facilitate the participation of reserve forces, civil defense corps, local residents, and others in integrated defense operations and training;
3. Preparedness measures for vulnerable areas;
4. Recommendations for awards for persons who have rendered distinguished service in integrated defense operations and training.
(8) Measures for the efficient fostering, operation, and support of national defense elements under Article 5 (3) 5 of the Act shall include the following: <Amended on December 24, 2018>
1. Matters concerning the establishment and maintenance of company offices of local reserve forces;
2. Matters concerning the enhancement of the morale of members of national defense elements who have participated in integrated defense operations and training, and the strengthening of ties among civilians, the Government, and the military.

Article 9 (Scope of Workplaces Required to Establish Workplace Councils)
The scope of workplaces required to establish workplace integrated defense councils under Article 6 (2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "Workplace Councils") shall be as follows:
1. Workplaces in which reserve force units at company level or above have been organized [a workplace having reserve force personnel sufficient for a platoon-level workplace reserve force unit may also establish a Workplace Council if it so desires];
2. Workplaces that are critical national facilities designated under Article 21 (4) of the Act.

Article 10 (Composition and Operation of Workplace Councils, etc.)
(1) A Workplace Council shall consist of persons designated by the chairperson from among the commander of the reserve force unit of the relevant workplace and the executives of that workplace.
(2) A Workplace Council shall deliberate on the following matters:
1. Formulation and implementation of workplace-level defense measures and plans for support thereof;
2. Operation, fostering, and support of workplace reserve forces.
(3) Meetings of a Workplace Council shall be classified as regular meetings and extraordinary meetings. In principle, regular meetings shall be convened once each quarter, and extraordinary meetings shall be convened when the chairperson deems it necessary. Resolutions at a meeting shall be adopted with the attendance of a majority of its incumbent members and the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.

Article 11 (Industrial Complex Councils)
(1) Where reserve force personnel from workplaces within the same industrial complex (meaning a free trade zone under the Act on the Designation and Operation of Free Trade Zones, an industrial complex under the Industrial Sites and Development Act, or an important industrial facility designated by the Minister of Trade and Industry; hereinafter the same shall apply) are integrated to organize a reserve force unit, an industrial complex integrated defense council (hereinafter referred to as an "Industrial Complex Council") may be established within that industrial complex. <Amended on March 23, 2013; October 1, 2025>
(2) Where an Industrial Complex Council is established, its chairperson shall be the representative responsible for managing the industrial complex under the Industrial Cluster Development and Factory Establishment Act, the Act on the Designation and Operation of Free Trade Zones, the Industrial Sites and Development Act, or other relevant statutes. Its members shall be persons commissioned by the chairperson, including the commander of the industrial complex reserve force unit, officials of authorities related to the defense of the industrial complex, and other persons such as representatives of enterprises within the industrial complex.
(3) An Industrial Complex Council shall deliberate on the following matters:
1. Industrial-complex-level defense measures and plans for support thereof;
2. Fostering, operation, and expenses of the industrial complex reserve force unit.
(4) The relevant provisions concerning Regional Councils or Workplace Councils shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of Industrial Complex Councils.

Article 12 (Inspection and Supervision of Integrated Defense Preparedness, etc.)
(1) To effectively inspect and supervise integrated defense preparedness under Article 8 (3) 2 of the Act, the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall conduct a regular inspection or guidance visit of national defense elements once each year and shall conduct special inspections or guidance visits as necessary.
(2) Following deliberation by the Integrated Defense Working Committee under Article 8 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Integrated Defense Working Committee"), the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall recommend to the Government that awards be given to individuals, units, or institutions found, as a result of inspections or guidance visits under paragraph (1), to have rendered distinguished service in establishing an integrated defense posture, and to individuals, units, or institutions found, as a result of the comprehensive analysis of integrated defense operational situations under Article 8 (3) 3 of the Act, to have rendered distinguished service in integrated defense operations.

Article 13 (Composition and Operation of the Integrated Defense Working Committee, etc.)
(1) The chairperson of the Integrated Defense Working Committee shall be the Deputy Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters, and its members shall be persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Officers at department head level at the Joint Chiefs of Staff designated by the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters;
2. One affiliated public official at director-general level designated by each member of the Central Council;
3. One affiliated public official at director-general level designated by the head of each relevant authority.
(2) The Integrated Defense Working Committee shall deliberate on the following matters:
1. Integrated defense preparedness measures;
2. Coordination of integrated defense affairs among government ministries;
3. Amendment of statutes and regulations concerning integrated defense;
4. Awards under Article 12 (2) and requests for disciplinary action under Article 23 of the Act.
(3) Meetings of the Integrated Defense Working Committee shall be classified as regular meetings and extraordinary meetings. In principle, regular meetings shall be convened once each quarter, and extraordinary meetings shall be convened when the chairperson deems it necessary. Resolutions at a meeting shall be adopted with the attendance of a majority of its incumbent members and the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.
(4) Matters necessary for the organization and operation of the Integrated Defense Working Committee, other than those prescribed in this Decree, shall be determined by the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters.

Article 14 (Composition of Integrated Defense General Situation Rooms)
The integrated defense general situation room under Article 9 (2) 2 of the Act shall consist of the situation room of each integrated defense support headquarters and a joint military and police situation room (hereinafter referred to as a "joint situation room").

Article 15 (Criteria for Establishing Integrated Defense General Situation Rooms)
(1) As a rule, a joint situation room shall be established at each integrated defense support headquarters; provided, where it is established separately, it shall be established at the most effective location in a military unit or national police office, taking into account the ease of command, communications, and coordination, the characteristics of the area, and other relevant factors.
(2) Where a single military unit or police station has jurisdiction over two or more adjoining Sis/Guns or autonomous Gus, the joint situation rooms of those Sis/Guns or autonomous Gus may be combined and established at one location.

Article 16 (Criteria for Operating Integrated Defense General Situation Rooms)
(1) An integrated defense general situation room shall operate when a state of integrated defense has been declared and when major training exercises to establish an integrated defense posture are conducted.
(2) A joint situation room shall operate under the responsibility of the head of the military unit responsible for operations in the relevant area or the head of the national police office for that area.
(3) Matters necessary for the operation of an integrated defense general situation room, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the chairperson of the Regional Council following deliberation by that Council.

Article 17 (Functions of Integrated Defense Support Headquarters)
The functions of each integrated defense support headquarters under Article 9 (2) 5 of the Act (referring to the City/Do integrated defense support headquarters and the Si/Gun/Gu/Eup/Myeon/Dong integrated defense support headquarters under Article 9 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall be as follows:
1. Supporting mobilization duties related to integrated defense operations;
2. Supporting the establishment of regional joint press headquarters under Article 20 (2);
3. Implementing matters deliberated and resolved upon by the Regional Council.

Article 18 (Criteria for the Organization of Integrated Defense Support Headquarters)
(1) Each integrated defense support headquarters shall consist of a situation room and support teams for individual fields.
(2) The support teams for individual fields under paragraph (1) shall cover overall coordination; mobilization of personnel and financial resources; mobilization of industry, transportation, and equipment; medical services and relief; supplies and meals; communications and information technology; public relations; and other fields, but may be adjusted to suit the characteristics of each area.
(3) The head of each integrated defense support headquarters shall be the deputy head of the relevant local government in the case of a Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, Special Self-Governing Province, Si, Gun, or autonomous Gu, and shall be the head of the Eup, Myeon, or Dong, respectively, in the case of an Eup, Myeon, or Dong. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>
(4) Each integrated defense support headquarters shall be located at the principal office of the relevant Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, Special Self-Governing Province, Si, Gun, autonomous Gu, Eup, Myeon, or Dong. <Amended on Dec. 24, 2018>

Article 18-2 (Establishment and Operation of Intelligence Centers and Joint Intelligence Investigation Teams)
(1) The Director of the National Intelligence Service shall establish and operate intelligence centers under Article 9-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "intelligence centers") and joint intelligence investigation teams under paragraph (2) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "joint intelligence investigation teams") according to the following classifications:
1. Intelligence centers: Intelligence centers at the level of a Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as "City/Do" in this paragraph), and intelligence centers at the Si/Gun/Gu level (Gu means an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article);
2. Joint intelligence investigation teams: A central joint intelligence investigation team, joint intelligence investigation teams at the City/Do level, and joint intelligence investigation teams at the Si/Gun/Gu level.
(2) The National Intelligence Service shall serve as the coordinating agency for intelligence centers and joint intelligence investigation teams; provided, the President may designate a different coordinating agency for intelligence centers and joint intelligence investigation teams established in Seoul Special Metropolitan City and Si/Gun/Gu areas.
(3) Details concerning the establishment and operation of intelligence centers and joint intelligence investigation teams, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the President.

Article 19 (Support for News Gathering)
(1) To support news gathering under Article 10 (1) of the Act, the commander of integrated defense operations (referring to the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters, a regional military commander, a fleet commander, or the commissioner of a City/Do police agency; hereinafter the same shall apply) shall provide the press corps with information on the progress of integrated defense operations at least once a day through the joint press headquarters under Article 10 (2) of the Act. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2020>
(2) A news organization wishing to report on the progress of integrated defense operations shall notify the commander of integrated defense operations of its list of reporters, and the commander of integrated defense operations shall, unless there are special circumstances, provide the reporters on that list with identification marks designated by that commander.
(3) The commander of integrated defense operations shall guarantee freedom of news gathering to reporters bearing the identification marks under paragraph (2) within the areas designated by that commander as areas where news gathering is permitted; provided, the commander of integrated defense operations may restrict news gathering where that commander considers it to interfere with integrated defense operations.
(4) A reporter wishing to conduct on-site reporting outside the areas designated by the commander of integrated defense operations as areas where news gathering is permitted shall obtain approval from that commander and conduct such reporting under the guidance of a guide provided by that commander. In such cases, the commander of integrated defense operations may approve on-site reporting only for one or more selected representatives.
(5) Depending on the circumstances and progress of integrated defense operations, the commander of integrated defense operations may, where necessary for the efficient conduct of integrated defense operations, withhold disclosure of the details of specific acts of infiltration or provocation by the enemy, the status of integrated defense operations by friendly forces, and other such information for the necessary period.

Article 20 (Criteria for Establishing Joint Press Headquarters)
(1) Upon the declaration of a state of integrated defense, the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall establish central joint press headquarters within the Integrated Defense Headquarters pursuant to Article 10 (2) of the Act.
(2) Where a regional military commander, a fleet commander, or the commissioner of a City/Do police agency is designated as the commander of integrated defense operations for an area in which a state of integrated defense has been declared, that commander or commissioner shall establish regional joint press headquarters pursuant to Article 10 (2) of the Act. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2020>
(3) The regional joint press headquarters under paragraph (2) shall be established at each integrated defense support headquarters or in an area adjacent to an integrated defense operations command post; provided, where necessary, field joint press headquarters may be established in an area where news gathering is easy.

CHAPTER III ALERT POSTURE AND STATES OF INTEGRATED DEFENSE <Newly Inserted on Nov. 17, 2009>

Article 21 (Issuance and Lifting of Alert Posture)
(1) "Heads of military units and heads of police offices prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 11 (1) of the Act means the persons falling under the following classifications:
1. Areas outside Seoul Special Metropolitan City:
(a) Commanders at regimental commander level or above (referring to independent squadron commander level in the case of the Navy and Air Force);
(b) Commanders at police station chief level or above;
2. Seoul Special Metropolitan City: Heads of military units designated by the President.
(2) A commander or head of a military unit falling under the classifications in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as an "issuing authority") shall issue or lift an alert posture after reporting to the immediate superior commander, unless there is an unavoidable reason, such as an interruption of communications.
(3) Immediately upon issuing or lifting an alert posture, an issuing authority shall notify all national defense elements within the area under its jurisdiction of that fact and report it to or notify the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters.

Article 22 (Types of Alert Posture)
(1) An alert posture under Article 11 (1) of the Act may be issued as a Level 3 alert posture, Level 2 alert posture, or Level 1 alert posture, taking into account the circumstances of enemy infiltration or provocation.
(2) The details of the classification of alert postures under paragraph (1), the measures to be taken, and other relevant matters shall be determined by the President.

Article 23 (Command and Cooperation Relationships upon Issuance of an Alert Posture)
(1) Upon the issuance of a Level 1 alert posture pursuant to Article 11 (1) of the Act and Article 22 (1) of this Decree, command and cooperation relationships among national defense elements shall be as follows: <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014, Jul. 26, 2017, Dec. 31, 2020>
1. Areas under police jurisdiction: The commissioner of a City/Do police agency shall conduct joint military and police operations by exercising operational control (meaning assigning operational missions for integrated defense to, and issuing directions to, units, departments, or agencies under command; hereinafter the same shall apply) over the military operational elements entrusted by the regional military commander;
2. Areas under military jurisdiction: The regional military commander shall conduct joint military and police operations by exercising operational control over the police operational elements entrusted by the commissioner of a City/Do police agency;
3. Specific security areas: The regional military commander shall conduct operations by exercising operational control over all national defense elements in the relevant area;
4. Specific security waters and general security waters: The fleet commander shall conduct joint military and police operations by exercising operational control over coast guard personnel in the waters under the fleet commander's jurisdiction;
5. The head of a coastal security unit shall perform duties by exercising operational control over coast guard personnel working at agencies receiving reports on the entry and departure of vessels;
6. Heads of local governments and heads of Eups, Myeons, and Dongs shall support operations through their respective integrated defense support headquarters.
(2) Upon the issuance of a Level 2 or Level 3 alert posture pursuant to Article 11 (1) of the Act and Article 22 (1) of this Decree, national defense elements shall cooperate with one another to prepare against enemy infiltration or provocation.
(3) Regional military commanders, commissioners of City/Do police agencies, fleet commanders, and commissioners of regional coast guard agencies shall, beginning in peacetime, formulate their respective operational plans in coordination with one another to prepare against enemy infiltration or provocation. <Amended on Nov. 19, 2014, Jul. 26, 2017, Dec. 31, 2020>

Article 23-2

Article 24 (Procedures for Declaring and Lifting States of Integrated Defense)
(1) The declaration or lifting of a state of integrated defense pursuant to Article 12 (3) and (5) and Article 14 (1) and (4) of the Act shall require the attendance of a majority of all incumbent members of the Central Council or the City/Do Council and the affirmative vote of at least two-thirds of the members present.
(2) Where the authority empowered to declare or lift a state of integrated defense declares or lifts such a state pursuant to Article 12 (3) and (5) and Article 14 (1) and (4) of the Act, it shall notify the heads of the relevant local governments in writing of the declaration or lifting, require them to publicly announce that fact through the bulletin boards of the Sis, Guns, autonomous Gus, Eups, Myeons, and Dongs in the relevant area, and arrange for that fact to be reported by newspapers and broadcasters.

CHAPTER IV INTEGRATED DEFENSE OPERATIONS AND TRAINING <Newly Inserted on Nov. 17, 2009>

Article 25 (Command and Cooperation Relationships upon Declaration of a State of Integrated Defense)
(1) The command structure by level of state of integrated defense and by area of jurisdiction under Article 15 (3) of the Act shall be as follows: <Amended on Dec. 31, 2020>
1. Where a Class A state has been declared: The Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters or the regional military commander shall conduct integrated defense operations by exercising operational control over all national defense elements;
2. Where a Class B state has been declared: The regional military commander shall conduct integrated defense operations by exercising operational control over all national defense elements;
3. Where a Class C state has been declared:
(a) Areas under police jurisdiction: The commissioner of a City/Do police agency shall conduct integrated defense operations by exercising operational control over civil defense corps resources and military operational elements entrusted by the regional military commander;
(b) Specific security areas and areas under military jurisdiction: The regional military commander shall conduct integrated defense operations by exercising operational control over all national defense elements within the area under the commander's jurisdiction;
(c) Specific security waters and general security waters: The fleet commander shall conduct integrated defense operations by exercising operational control over all national defense elements within the waters under the commander's jurisdiction.
(2) Where a state of integrated defense has been declared, heads of local governments shall establish an integrated defense posture at the regional level by linking the public with national defense elements, supporting integrated defense operations, and taking other such measures in cooperation with regional military commanders, fleet commanders, and commissioners of City/Do police agencies pursuant to Article 15 (3) of the Act. <Amended on Dec. 31, 2020>
(3) Commanders of integrated defense operations shall be responsible for integrated defense operations as a whole and shall formulate and implement their respective plans for integrated defense operations in which national defense elements are integrated and coordinated with one another.
(4) The commissioner of a City/Do police agency conducting integrated defense operations pursuant to paragraph (1) 3 may request operational support from the regional military commander where necessary for the efficient conduct of integrated defense operations. <Newly Inserted on Aug. 20, 2013, Dec. 31, 2020>
(5) A regional military commander requested to provide operational support pursuant to paragraph (4) may organize a military operational support team to provide such support. <Newly Inserted on Aug. 20, 2013>
(6) Where a state of integrated defense has been declared, managers of critical national facilities shall strengthen their own guarding, security, and protective measures, formulate and implement countermeasures against enemy infiltration, and establish protective readiness in cooperation with commanders of battalion-level units responsible for the relevant areas and chiefs of police stations. <Amended on Aug. 20, 2013>
(7) Where a state of integrated defense has been declared, the Minister of the Interior and Safety, the Director of the National Intelligence Service, the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency, and the Commissioner General of the Korea Coast Guard shall appoint public officials of Grade V or higher as liaison officers and station them in the military situation room of the Integrated Defense Headquarters to maintain a system of cooperation among national defense elements, and other relevant agencies shall dispatch liaison officers where requested by the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters. <Amended on Mar. 23, 2013, Aug. 20, 2013, Nov. 19, 2014, Jul. 26, 2017>

Article 26 (Procedures for Stopping and Questioning Persons, etc.)
(1) A person performing a mission in integrated defense operations (hereinafter referred to as a "person performing an operational mission") may, pursuant to Article 15(5) of the Act, stop and question a person who is acting suspiciously or whom there are substantial grounds to suspect of acting suspiciously upon a reasonable assessment of the surrounding circumstances.
(2) Where it is deemed that questioning a person under paragraph (1) at the place concerned would be disadvantageous to that person or would interfere with traffic or integrated defense operations, the person may be requested to accompany the person performing the operational mission to a nearby checkpoint, military unit, or national police office for questioning.
(3) A person performing an operational mission may, when questioning a person pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2), check whether that person is carrying a dangerous weapon or firearm.
(4) When questioning a person or requesting that a person accompany them pursuant to paragraph (1) or (2), a person performing an operational mission shall disclose their status, affiliation, position, and name and explain the purpose and reasons therefor, and shall specify the destination when requesting that the person accompany them.

Article 27 (Criteria for Establishing Controlled Zones, etc.)
(1) A controlled zone under Article 16(1) of the Act shall be established as an area that can minimize harm to residents and ensure the efficiency of integrated defense operations, in accordance with the following criteria:
1. An area where engagement in combat or the like may endanger life or bodily safety;
2. An area where combat is anticipated and restrictions on access by persons other than operational personnel are required;
3. Any other area where access by persons other than operational personnel is likely to interfere with integrated defense operations.
(2) To establish a controlled zone, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, a Special Self-Governing Province Governor, the mayor of a Si, the head of a Gun, or the head of an autonomous Gu (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor, etc.") shall, upon the recommendation of the operations commander, first undergo deliberation by the Regional Council. <Amended 2018.12.24>
(3) Upon establishing a controlled zone, a Mayor/Do Governor, etc. shall specify the duration, area, and reasons for its establishment; the details of prohibitions, restrictions, and orders to leave the controlled zone; the penalties for persons violating them; and other relevant matters, and shall notify the heads of the relevant local governments within their jurisdiction in writing, publicly announce the establishment on the bulletin boards of the Si, Gun, autonomous Gu, Eup, Myeon, or Dong in which the controlled zone is located, and arrange for it to be reported in newspapers and broadcasts.

Article 27-2

Article 28 (Methods for Issuing Evacuation Orders)
The "methods prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 17(2) of the Act mean the following methods: <Amended 2018.12.24>
1. Broadcasts by television, radio, cable broadcasting, or the like;
2. Publication in national and local daily newspapers;
3. Posting on the website of the relevant local government;
4. Posting on the website of an information and communications service provider under Article 2(1)3 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection;
5. Posting on social network services;
6. Distribution of leaflets;
7. Oral communication through emergency contact networks;
8. Ringing bells, sounding horns, or hoisting signal flags;
9. Emergency mobile phone text messages.

Article 29 (Methods and Procedures for Implementing Evacuation Orders)
(1) To implement an evacuation order under Article 17 of the Act, the operations commander shall select the area from which residents, etc. need to evacuate and make a recommendation to the Mayor/Do Governor, etc.
(2) A Mayor/Do Governor, etc. who receives a recommendation from the operations commander under paragraph (1) shall review its appropriateness, determine the evacuation area, publicly announce the evacuation order by the methods specified in Article 17(2) of the Act and the subparagraphs of Article 28 of this Decree, and then request the operations commander to execute the evacuation order.
(3) An operations commander who receives a request to execute an evacuation order under paragraph (2) shall evacuate residents and persons staying in the evacuation area by using national defense elements, including civilians, government authorities, the police, the military, and reserve forces.
(4) Residents and persons staying in the evacuation area shall take measures to prevent supplies and equipment from being used by the enemy for infiltration or acts of provocation, and then promptly evacuate in accordance with the instructions of the operations commander.

Article 30 (Methods for Safe Evacuation)
(1) For safe evacuation under Article 17(3) of the Act, the operations commander shall designate safe evacuation routes, times, methods, and areas, and then station guidance personnel along the boundary of the evacuation area and, in the case of evacuation at sea, deploy guide vessels or maintain communications networks.
(2) Residents and persons staying in the evacuation area shall evacuate to a safe area in accordance with the instructions of the guidance personnel under paragraph (1), signals from guide vessels, or instructions communicated over the communications networks.
(3) Identification markings for the guidance personnel and guide vessels under paragraph (1) shall be as prescribed by the operations commander.

Article 31 (Establishment and Operation of Checkpoints, etc.)
(1) Deleted. <2014.11.19>
(2) The commissioner of a City/Do police agency, the commissioner of a regional coast guard agency, the regional military commander, and the fleet commander (hereinafter referred to as the "commissioner of a City/Do police agency, etc.") may, pursuant to Article 18 of the Act, establish and operate checkpoints at strategic land and sea transportation points within their jurisdictions, such as airports and ports where enemy infiltration is anticipated. In such cases, the commissioner of a City/Do police agency may, where necessary, receive support from reserve forces provided by the regional military commander to establish and operate checkpoints in vulnerable areas. <Amended 2014.11.19, 2016.11.29, 2017.7.26, 2020.12.31>
(3) Before establishing or closing a joint police and military checkpoint pursuant to paragraph (2), the commissioner of a City/Do police agency, etc. shall report to, or notify, the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters in advance. <Amended 2020.12.31>
(4) To establish a joint checkpoint, the commissioner of a City/Do police agency, etc. shall take necessary preparedness measures in advance, including establishing wired and wireless communications networks linking the checkpoint with national police offices and military units in neighboring areas and securing barriers. <Amended 2020.12.31>
(5) When establishing and operating a joint checkpoint, the commissioner of a City/Do police agency, etc. shall clearly define the respective chains of command of the police and the military and actively cooperate with one another to ensure that stopping and questioning persons and other duties are performed smoothly and effectively. <Amended 2020.12.31>

CHAPTER V MANAGEMENT OF CRITICAL NATIONAL FACILITIES AND VULNERABLE AREAS <Newly Inserted 2009.11.17>

Article 32 (Guarding, Security, and Protection of Critical National Facilities)
For the guarding, security, and protection of critical national facilities, managers of critical national facilities (including their owners; hereinafter the same shall apply), commissioners of City/Do police agencies, regional military commanders, and commanders of battalion-level units with territorial responsibility shall perform the duties specified in the following subparagraphs: <Amended 2016.11.29, 2020.10.7, 2020.12.31>
1. In the case of managers, the following duties:
(a) Formulation and implementation of self-protection plans that integrate protection personnel, such as police assigned for special guard, special security guards, workplace reserve forces, and workplace civil defense corps, with obstacles and scientific surveillance equipment. In such cases, the self-protection plans shall include matters concerning job training on legislation governing integrated defense and the guarding, security, and protection of facilities, as well as live-fire training using individual firearms, conducted under the responsibility of the managers and special security business operators;
(b) Taking necessary preparedness measures for the self-protection of critical national facilities, including establishing integrated situation rooms and command and communications networks;
2. In the case of commissioners of City/Do police agencies and regional military commanders, formulation and implementation of protection support plans for critical national facilities within their jurisdictions that integrate national defense elements, such as the military, the police, reserve forces, and civil defense corps. In such cases, the police shall formulate and implement protection support plans at police station level, and the military shall formulate and implement protection support plans at battalion level;
3. Managers, commanders of battalion-level units with territorial responsibility, and chiefs of police stations shall enter into agreements on the allocation of roles and other matters for the protection of critical national facilities, and shall notify one another when formulating or revising self-protection plans or protection support plans at battalion or police station level.

Article 33 (Definition of Open Areas or Lakes)
Open areas or lakes under Article 22(1)3 of the Act shall meet the following criteria:
1. A width of at least 30 meters and a length of at least 250 meters (a length of at least 200 meters where there are no obstacles at either end in the longitudinal direction);
2. For open areas, a gradient of no more than 12 degrees in the forward direction and no more than 5 degrees in either lateral direction;
3. For lakes, a water depth of at least 80 centimeters.

Article 34 (Designation of Vulnerable Areas and Revocation of Designation, etc.)
(1) The "areas prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 22(1)4 of the Act mean areas, such as sea areas, coasts, and islands, where the enemy can easily infiltrate or operate while in hiding.
(2) Each year, regional military commanders, fleet commanders, and commissioners of City/Do police agencies shall review and analyze, for areas within their jurisdictions falling under any subparagraph of Article 22(1) of the Act, the characteristics of the terrain, factors rendering them vulnerable to enemy infiltration, and the actual changes in the integrated defense environment resulting from regional development, and shall notify the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, the relevant Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") of the findings. <Amended 2018.12.24, 2020.12.31>
(3) When designating a vulnerable area or revoking the designation of a vulnerable area pursuant to Article 22(1) of the Act, a Mayor/Do Governor shall consider the findings notified under paragraph (2) and the results of their own analysis of the area, and shall undergo deliberation by the City/Do Council.

Article 35 (Installation of Barriers and Restrictions on Access)
(1) Before installing barriers in a vulnerable area to restrict civilian access pursuant to Article 22(4) of the Act, the regional military commander shall notify the Mayor/Do Governor in advance, and shall attach the signs set out in the Annex to barriers, such as wire fences, at intervals of no more than 300 meters.
(2) The regional military commander may impose access restrictions on civilians seeking to enter a vulnerable area where barriers have been installed pursuant to paragraph (1), after verifying their personal details, purposes of access, and areas to be accessed.

Article 36 (Standards for Integrated Defense Preparedness Measures in Vulnerable Areas)
An ordinance of a City/Do prescribing integrated defense preparedness measures for vulnerable areas pursuant to Article 22(5) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended 2022.11.1>
1. The following general matters:
(a) Annual plans for the construction of roads in vulnerable areas;
(b) Securing and maintaining communications networks for integrated defense operations;
(c) Organization of resident reporting networks within vulnerable areas;
(d) Public education and publicity activities on enemy infiltration tactics and reporting procedures in cooperation with relevant agencies;
(e) Periodic reporting drills for identifying and reporting persons acting suspiciously;
(f) Conducting medical outreach and volunteer activities for residents of vulnerable areas;
2. Installation, in open areas under Article 33, of obstacles falling under any of the following items. In such cases, the obstacles shall conform to the specifications prescribed by the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters, and provision shall be made to allow consultation with the regional military commander where necessary for their operation:
(a) Standing trees at least 10 years old;
(b) Sand bunkers or ponds;
(c) Movable obstacles (meaning barricades, steel spikes, wire ropes positioned at a height of at least 1 meter and 50 centimeters, or vehicles, etc. effective as obstacles);
(d) Other structures, such as sports or cultural facilities, that can be used as obstacles;
3. Implementation of preparedness measures, such as water patrol activities conducted independently, for lakes under Article 33.

CHAPTER VI SUPPLEMENTARY PROVISIONS <Newly Inserted 2009.11.17>

Article 37 (Requests for Disciplinary Action, etc.)
(1) Cases in which neglect of duties has caused serious hindrance to national security or integrated defense affairs under Article 23(1) of the Act shall be any of the following:
1. Where a person evades integrated defense operations or exercises without justifiable grounds, causing serious hindrance to such operations or exercises;
2. Where a person participating in integrated defense operations or exercises intentionally evades the operations or exercises and disobeys control, causing serious hindrance to the exercises or operations or losses in combat power, such as personnel, equipment, or facilities;
3. Where a person participating in integrated defense operations, intentionally or through gross negligence, causes the delay, alteration, or failure of integrated defense operations, such as by allowing the enemy to escape or go into hiding as a result of inadequate response measures, including false or delayed reporting or insufficient immediate response;
4. Any other case where a public official responsible for integrated defense affairs neglects those affairs, causing serious hindrance to integrated defense affairs, such as by allowing weaknesses to arise in the integrated defense posture.
(2) The Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters may direct public officials under the Chief's authority to conduct investigations necessary for requesting disciplinary action pursuant to Article 23 of the Act.
(3) Where necessary for an investigation under paragraph (2), the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters may request a joint investigation with the agencies to which the persons concerned belong or with relevant agencies, or may request necessary materials.
(4) Upon receiving reports on the results of investigations under paragraphs (2) and (3) and other relevant matters, the Chief of the Integrated Defense Headquarters shall decide whether to request disciplinary action against the person concerned following deliberation by the Integrated Defense Working Committee.

